# How to cite Picasso. GitHub shows it as "Cite this repository". Only
# release 1.0.0 is deposited on Zenodo.
#
# When the Picasso 1.0 paper is published:
# 1. Make it the preferred-citation below and move the Nature Protocols
#    paper to the references.
# 2. Update the other places that name the paper: the "Citing Picasso"
#    section and the introduction of readme.rst (then run
#    release/sync_installer_readmes.py for the installer read-me files),
#    the citation card and the introduction of docs/index.rst, the DOI
#    badge of readme.rst and "Publications" in pyproject.toml.
#    tests/test_citation.py lists the places that still lack the
#    preferred-citation's DOI.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Picasso in your research, please cite the publication below.
  Many features are based on further publications, see
  https://picassosr.readthedocs.io/en/latest/getting-started/citing.html.
type: software
title: Picasso
abstract: >-
  A collection of tools for painting super-resolution images, covering
  single-molecule localization microscopy (SMLM) analysis from raw movies
  to localization, rendering and quantification.
authors:
  - family-names: Kowalewski
    given-names: Rafal
  - family-names: Schnitzbauer
    given-names: Joerg
  - family-names: Strauss
    given-names: Maximilian T.
  - name: Jungmann Lab, Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry
repository-code: https://github.com/jungmannlab/picasso
url: https://picassosr.readthedocs.io/en/latest/
license: MIT
keywords:
  - super-resolution microscopy
  - single-molecule localization microscopy
  - DNA-PAINT
preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: Super-resolution microscopy with DNA-PAINT
  authors:
    - family-names: Schnitzbauer
      given-names: Joerg
    - family-names: Strauss
      given-names: Maximilian T.
  journal: Nature Protocols
  volume: 12
  issue: 6
  start: 1198
  end: 1228
  year: 2017
  doi: 10.1038/nprot.2017.024
